WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:09.340
The way marketing teams work is changing.
Your design tools, your CMS, your

00:09.340 --> 00:14.040
analytics platform haven't gone anywhere,
but more and more of the actual work is

00:14.040 --> 00:18.980
happening inside a chat window. And agents
are increasingly becoming members of your

00:18.980 --> 00:23.740
extended team. But the stakes in the web
are higher than writing in internal doc.

00:24.400 --> 00:28.180
When an agent makes a change to your
website, it's visible to every customer,

00:28.180 --> 00:33.780
prospects, and AI search engine that
discovers you. Marketing teams now have to

00:33.780 --> 00:38.540
move at the speed of AI without losing the
governance, control, and scale that

00:38.540 --> 00:44.320
production sites demand. At Webflow, we're
building the future of marketing, one

00:44.320 --> 00:48.120
where every digital experience is
continuously growing, continuously getting

00:48.120 --> 00:53.040
better, different for every person who
touches it, but unmistakably the same

00:53.040 --> 00:59.380
brand underneath. Today marks a major step
towards that future with Webflow MCP 2.0.

00:59.720 --> 01:04.400
I can't wait for you all to see how it
works. Keegan, take it away. Webflow has

01:04.400 --> 01:08.320
always given teams everything that they
need to build confidently at scale, design

01:08.320 --> 01:13.560
tools, governance, and the platform
underneath. MCP 2.0 is how we're bringing

01:13.560 --> 01:18.880
that same foundation to the way teams
build with AI today. For some of you, this

01:18.880 --> 01:22.820
will be your first introduction to what
Webflow's MCP can do. For the growing

01:22.820 --> 01:26.700
number of you already building with it,
you're gonna see some big improvements. So

01:26.700 --> 01:32.960
let's get into it. For those who aren't
already familiar, Webflow's MCP server

01:32.960 --> 01:36.680
makes it easy to connect Webflow to AI
tools like Claude and Cursor, so you can

01:36.680 --> 01:40.980
design, build, and manage your sites
through natural conversation or full-agent

01:40.980 --> 01:46.480
workflows. With MCP 2.0, we're making that
experience a lot more powerful, so you can

01:46.480 --> 01:50.840
move faster with the control and
governance that you need to scale. The

01:50.840 --> 01:55.880
first big update is deeper support for how
teams build and update pages. Agents can

01:55.880 --> 02:00.820
now create and manage components with full
support for props, variants, and slots.

02:01.520 --> 02:06.160
They can also connect components and
elements to CMS data. So when you ask an

02:06.160 --> 02:10.060
AI tool to build something, it can deliver
production-ready outputs that use your

02:10.060 --> 02:14.640
existing design system and work with
dynamic data. Let's start with a prompt to

02:14.640 --> 02:19.600
build a new CMS collection page using our
site's existing design system. We can go

02:19.600 --> 02:24.200
even further by asking you to import CMS
items to populate that collection page and

02:24.200 --> 02:29.080
bind the data to fields that connect the
content. I now have a dynamic page that is

02:29.080 --> 02:34.140
on-brand and ready to publish. Also,
because it's built in Webflow, this is a

02:34.140 --> 02:39.040
production-ready page supported by all the
tools you need to manage, optimize, and

02:39.040 --> 02:45.320
scale it from day one. AI can help you
build fast by default, but getting output

02:45.320 --> 02:50.340
that's fast and yours requires providing
your agents the right context. What your

02:50.340 --> 02:54.260
brand sounds like, how to apply your
design system, what your legal team will

02:54.260 --> 02:59.500
care about the most. For most teams, this
context exists, but it's fragmented. It's

02:59.500 --> 03:03.840
not readily available for agents to
reference. Agent instructions fixes that.

03:04.520 --> 03:09.620
Now, your team can create and store your
context once, directly in Webflow, and

03:09.620 --> 03:13.860
your agents can reference it wherever they
work. Agent instructions can include

03:13.860 --> 03:18.180
anything from your design system resources
to external references like legal policies

03:18.180 --> 03:23.620
or voice and tone guidelines. Once that
context is in place, your agents have the

03:23.620 --> 03:28.020
same information as the rest of your team,
and the work they produce actually shows

03:28.020 --> 03:34.860
it. MCP 2.0 also makes your analyzed data
accessible directly inside of your AI

03:34.860 --> 03:38.880
workflows. You can ask an agent how your
site is performing this week compared to

03:38.880 --> 03:44.300
last week. Things like traffic, top pages,
sessions by channel, all of it, and you

03:44.300 --> 03:49.160
get answers without leaving your chat. But
the real power is what you can do with the

03:49.160 --> 03:52.680
data once you have it. Because the MCP
connects Webflow to the rest of your

03:52.680 --> 03:56.560
stack, you can pull site performance data
directly into the tools that your team

03:56.560 --> 04:01.420
already works in. Share a weekly summary
to Slack, push data into amplitude, or

04:01.420 --> 04:05.660
feed insights into whatever workflow makes
sense for your team. Of course, giving

04:05.660 --> 04:09.700
agents more access and the right context
is only part of the equation. The other

04:09.700 --> 04:16.420
part is making sure they're working within
the right boundaries. MCP 2.0 works with

04:16.420 --> 04:20.300
the same roles and permissions that you've
already configured in Webflow. It respects

04:20.300 --> 04:24.800
custom roles, granular access per site,
per CMS collection, per page, and per

04:24.800 --> 04:29.180
locale. If a teammate connects Cloud
through the MCP, they can only do what

04:29.180 --> 04:34.240
their Webflow role allows them to do. And
just like page branching has enabled teams

04:34.240 --> 04:39.000
to work on page updates safely, your agent
can now work on page branches as well, so

04:39.000 --> 04:42.980
that they're in an isolated environment
without touching production content. For

04:42.980 --> 04:47.000
example, you can work on homepage updates
in a branch to ensure your changes don't

04:47.000 --> 04:51.560
make it to your production site until
you're absolutely ready. Finally, every

04:51.560 --> 04:55.300
change made through Webflow MCP is now
logged in your site activity log with

04:55.300 --> 04:59.740
clear AI attribution. So you can see
exactly what was changed, when, and

04:59.740 --> 05:03.980
whether it was a person or an agent. You
can even query that log directly from your

05:03.980 --> 05:08.640
chat, so getting to the bottom of an issue
takes seconds, not hours. So to sum it up,

05:08.940 --> 05:13.860
MCP 2.0 empowers you to move faster with
everything that you need for what comes

05:13.860 --> 05:19.120
next. And it's free for everyone, so check
out the link below to learn more. There's

05:19.120 --> 05:22.020
more coming this summer. We'll see you at
Webflow.com.

